WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.120 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
A school garden needs a fence.

00:00:02.120 --> 00:00:06.160 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Would counting the squares inside tell us how much fencing
to buy?

00:00:06.160 --> 00:00:09.240 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Imagine walking all the way around its edge.

00:00:09.240 --> 00:00:12.736 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
That journey measures the boundary, which is called the
perimeter.

00:00:12.736 --> 00:00:17.193 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Our learning goal is to find the perimeter of regular and
irregular shapes.

00:00:17.193 --> 00:00:18.753 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Success criteria.

00:00:18.753 --> 00:00:21.713 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
I can trace the complete outside boundary.

00:00:21.713 --> 00:00:23.793 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
I can use matching length units.

00:00:23.793 --> 00:00:26.785 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
I can find missing lengths before adding sides.

00:00:26.785 --> 00:00:30.785 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Use string to trace a shape, then straighten the string
beside a ruler.

00:00:30.785 --> 00:00:33.785 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
This makes the idea of perimeter concrete.

00:00:33.785 --> 00:00:39.225 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
On a diagram, follow every outside side once and add those
lengths using matching units.

00:00:39.225 --> 00:00:43.375 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
An inside dividing line is not part of the outside boundary.

00:00:43.375 --> 00:00:47.775 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
A rectangular garden is seven metres long and four metres
wide.

00:00:47.775 --> 00:00:50.095 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Opposite sides have matching lengths.

00:00:50.095 --> 00:00:54.064 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Trace the four edges and predict the total before we add
them.

00:00:54.064 --> 00:00:57.904 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Seven plus four plus seven plus four is twenty-two metres.

00:00:57.904 --> 00:01:01.344 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
We can also add seven and four, then double eleven.

00:01:01.344 --> 00:01:05.070 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Both methods count the complete boundary exactly once.

00:01:05.070 --> 00:01:07.390 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Now trace an L-shaped garden.

00:01:07.390 --> 00:01:07.670 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Its top is eight metres and its left side is six.

00:01:07.670 --> 00:01:14.470 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
A three-by-two rectangle is missing from the lower right
corner.

00:01:14.470 --> 00:01:17.727 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Use subtraction to find the remaining straight lengths.

00:01:17.727 --> 00:01:22.727 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
The six outside edges measure eight, four, three, two, five
and six metres.

00:01:22.727 --> 00:01:24.927 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Their sum is twenty-eight metres.

00:01:24.927 --> 00:01:30.149 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
The inward corner still belongs to the outside boundary, so
include both of its edges.

00:01:30.149 --> 00:01:33.989 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Seven times four counts square units covering this
rectangle.

00:01:33.989 --> 00:01:35.949 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
It does not measure the fence.

00:01:35.949 --> 00:01:39.109 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
For perimeter, trace and add the outside lengths.

00:01:39.109 --> 00:01:43.434 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
A useful check is whether your answer has metres or square
metres.

00:01:43.434 --> 00:01:46.754 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
A rectangle is six metres by three metres.

00:01:46.754 --> 00:01:48.314 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
What is its perimeter?

00:01:48.314 --> 00:01:51.649 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Look at the model, and keep your prediction in mind.

00:01:51.649 --> 00:01:53.489 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Six plus three makes nine.

00:01:53.489 --> 00:01:56.369 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Double nine for the four sides: eighteen metres.

00:01:56.369 --> 00:01:59.901 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Check by tracing and adding six, three, six and three.

00:01:59.901 --> 00:02:03.701 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Perimeter is a length around the complete outside edge.

00:02:03.701 --> 00:02:08.141 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Trace before calculating, find hidden lengths and keep units
consistent.

00:02:08.141 --> 00:02:13.981 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Try outlining a book with string, then compare a direct
measurement with the total of its side lengths.

00:02:13.981 --> 00:02:16.461 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Reflect on which model helped you and why.
